{"content":"# `_turnAnchor` and `_scrollTopMax` are body-identical — collapse them

Two new private methods with byte-equal bodies:

```dart
int _turnAnchor(List<String> wrapped) {
  final bottom = _scrollBottom(wrapped);
  final start = _turnStartRow();
  return start > bottom ? start : bottom;
}

int _scrollTopMax(List<String> wrapped) {
  final bottom = _scrollBottom(wrapped);
  if (!followTail) return bottom;
  final start = _turnStartRow();
  return start > bottom ? start : bottom;
}
```

`_turnAnchor` is exactly `_scrollTopMax` minus the (always-true at its call
sites) `followTail` guard — every `_turnAnchor` caller re-attaches
`followTail: true` in the same expression. The two names encode different
*intents* (stream re-anchor vs. user-scroll ceiling) and today cannot drift,
but nothing stops a future edit to one and not the other. Keep one as the
single implementation and express the other in terms of it, e.g.:

```dart
int _turnAnchor(List<String> wrapped) => _scrollTopMax(wrapped);
```

or extract the `max(start, bottom)` computation into one helper both call.
